==History==
The electorate was formed in [[1996 New Zealand general election|1996]] for the [[Mixed-member proportional representation|mixed-member proportional]] (MMP) voting system by combining the former [[Tasman (New Zealand electorate)|Tasman]] and [[West Coast (New Zealand electorate)|West Coast]] electorates.

[[Damien O'Connor]] was the first representative and he held the electorate until the [[2008 New Zealand general election|2008 general election]], when he was beaten by [[New Zealand National Party|National]] candidate [[Chris Auchinvole]], who had previously been a list MP. Auchinvole's majority was 971.<ref name="2008 West Coast-Tasman">{{cite web |title=Official Count Results – West Coast-Tasman |url= http://www.electionresults.govt.nz/electionresults_2008/electorate-60.html |publisher=Chief Electoral Office, Wellington|access-date=21 November 2011}}</ref> His position on the Labour Party list meant that O'Connor couldn't return to Parliament immediately. When the list MP [[Michael Cullen (politician)|Michael Cullen]] retired in May 2009, O'Connor regained his position as Member of the House of Representatives because he was the highest-ranked candidate on the list not already an MP.<ref>{{Cite news |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20090414075859/http://www.radionz.co.nz/news/stories/2009/04/13/1245a9f0a8a5 |archive-date=14 April 2009 |url-status= live |url= http://www.radionz.co.nz/news/stories/2009/04/13/1245a9f0a8a5 |title=O'Connor to return to Parliament |date=13 April 2009 |publisher=[[Radio New Zealand]]}}</ref> In contrast to the overall trend, he regained the electorate in the {{NZ election link|2011}}.<ref>{{cite web |title=O'Connor on course to grab West Coast seat|url= http://news.msn.co.nz/article/election/8380356/oconnor-on-course-to-grab-west-coast-seat |agency=Australian Associated Press |access-date=26 November 2011}}</ref>

Auchinvole retired from politics at the end of the 2011–2014 parliamentary term, and former Mayor of [[Westland District]], [[Maureen Pugh]], gained the nomination for the National Party.<ref>{{cite news |title=Pugh to seek Coast seat |url= http://www.stuff.co.nz/nelson-mail/news/9522862/Pugh-to-seek-Coast-seat |access-date=1 January 2014 |work=[[The Nelson Mail]] |date=16 December 2013}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |url= http://www.stuff.co.nz/the-press/news/west-coast/9522353/Former-Westland-mayor-to-stand-for-National |title=Former Westland mayor to stand for National |work=[[The Press]] |first=Glenn |last=Conway |date=16 December 2013}}</ref> O'Connor was once again successful. Based on preliminary results for the {{NZ election link|2014}}, Pugh was the lowest-ranked National Party list member who was returned to Parliament,<ref>{{cite news |last1=Mathewson |first1=Nicole |last2=Stylianou |first2=Georgina |last3=Fulton |first3=Tim |title=Election 2014: Canterbury decides |url= http://www.stuff.co.nz/the-press/news/10524525/Election-2014-Canterbury-decides |access-date=21 September 2014 |work=[[The Press]] |date=21 September 2014}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Farrar |first1=David |author-link1=David Farrar (blogger) |title=Election 2014: All the MPs for each party, plus those who failed to make the cut |url=http://www.nbr.co.nz/article/election-2014-all-mps-each-party-plus-those-who-failed-make-cut-ck-162749 |access-date=29 September 2014 |work=[[National Business Review]] |date=21 September 2014 |archive-date=23 September 2014 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140923232850/http://www.nbr.co.nz/article/election-2014-all-mps-each-party-plus-those-who-failed-make-cut-ck-162749 |url-status=dead }}</ref> but when the final results were released two weeks later, National had lost one list seat and Pugh did not get returned to Parliament.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Rutherford |first1=Hamish |title=National loses majority, Greens pick up one |url= http://www.stuff.co.nz/national/politics/election-results-2014/10580208/National-loses-majority-Greens-pick-up-one |access-date=4 October 2014 |work=[[Stuff.co.nz]] |date=4 October 2014}}</ref>

